<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calc2.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calc2-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calc2.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/resources/">Resources</a></li><li aria-current="page">Glossary</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker kicker-l">Reference</p><h1>Property Insurance Claim Glossary</h1><p class="lede">42 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type to filter.</p></div><svg class="ridge" viewBox="0 0 1440 90" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M0 90V62l110-30 60 22 130-50 90 44 60-16 120 38 150-62 80 34 90-20 130 44 110-34 70 18 140-48 100 40v48z"/></svg></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="gl-filter"><label for="gl-q">Search the glossary</label><input id="gl-q" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="gl-count" data-gl-count>42 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Jump to letter"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs are completed.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while your home is repaired after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-B">Bad faith</dt><dd>An insurer’s unreasonable conduct in handling a claim. A legal issue outside the appraisal process.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-C">Class 4 shingle</dt><dd>A shingle that earned the highest rating in a standard laboratory impact test. Not hail-proof.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Code upgrade</dt><dd>Work required by current building codes when repairing damage, such as ice barrier or ventilation improvements.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy condition that can reduce a payment if property is insured below a required percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Contents</dt><dd>Personal property such as furniture, clothing and electronics, usually Coverage C on a homeowners policy.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Cosmetic damage exclusion</dt><dd>An endorsement that excludes damage affecting appearance but not function, often for metal roofs and siding.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage — the limit for the house itself. Percentage deductibles are calculated from it.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page listing your coverages, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The part of a covered loss you pay before the insurer pays. May be a flat amount or a percentage.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition. Recoverable depreciation can be paid after repairs.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Downslope windstorm</dt><dd>Strong winds that accelerate down the lee side of the mountains, a frequent cause of Front Range wind damage.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-F">FAIR Plan</dt><dd>A last-resort insurance pool for owners who can’t get coverage in the standard market.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Fair rental value</dt><dd>Coverage for rent you lose when a rental property can’t be occupied after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Functional damage</dt><dd>Damage that impairs a material’s ability to perform, such as a fractured shingle mat.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-H">Holdback</dt><dd>The recoverable depreciation an insurer holds until repairs are completed and documented.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-I">Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the eaves that traps meltwater, which can back up under roofing and into the home.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Impartial appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser without a financial stake or bias in the outcome, as many policies require.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single priced entry in an estimate, such as “remove and replace laminated shingles, per square.”</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>Making repaired areas reasonably uniform in color, texture and appearance with undamaged areas.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or drying.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to comply with building codes and ordinances during repair or rebuilding.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Other structures</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures like garages, sheds and fences, usually Coverage B.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for coordinating a multi-trade repair, often around 10% each.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-P">Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible calculated as a percentage of Coverage A rather than a flat amount.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which an insurer may request within a set time.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the policy will pay once repairs or replacement are completed.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Roof payment schedule</dt><dd>An endorsement that pays roof losses based on roof age, often reducing payment significantly.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The full list of damaged items and the repairs needed — the foundation of any estimate.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Soft metals</dt><dd>Gutters, vents, flashing and similar metal components whose dents help show hail size and direction.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Square</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>Extra labor cost added for working on steeper roofs, typically 7/12 pitch and above.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy’s time limit for filing a lawsuit against the insurer.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an estimate after the initial inspection.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-T">Test square</dt><dd>A marked 10-by-10-foot roof area used to count hail hits on a slope.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third panel member who decides items the two appraisers can’t resolve.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt id="g-W">Waste factor</dt><dd>Extra material added to cover cutting and fitting, higher on complex roofs.</dd></div><div class="gl-item"><dt>Wildland-urban interface</dt><dd>Areas where homes meet or mix with undeveloped wildland vegetation, with elevated wildfire risk.</dd></div></dl><p class="gl-none" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
